887-### 0512 — A named digest algorithm is not a digest algorithm: hardened TCB completeness rested on two undefined constructions
888-
889-- ROOT CAUSE: `hardened-tcb-v1` required "two materially different trusted computing bases MUST NOT produce the same record" and identified each mutable component by `{kind, name, algorithm, content_sha256}`, but `curator-hardened-component-file-v1` and `curator-hardened-component-tree-v1` appeared only as enum values and two half-sentences (`protocol/hardened-execution.md:167,197,198`). Two implementations could hash different projections of one installed tree — following links or not, folding mode in or not — and both claim the same algorithm identity, so equal `content_sha256` did not mean equal component.
890-- ROOT CAUSE: section 8.5 stated the observed `tcb.backend.version` MUST be at or above a claim's `minimum_version`, and nothing compared them. Both were free `nonEmptyString`. Reviewer probe: observed `0` against declared `999999` gave 0 schema errors and `check_claim_qualification` ACCEPTED. Second probe: a linux claim whose `tcb.host.identity` was `windows` also passed — `host` was bound into the digest but related to no other member.
891:- FIX: `protocol/hardened-execution.md` §2.3.1 defines both constructions byte for byte in the `curator-build-source-v1` / `curator-go-toolchain-v1` house style; §2.3.2 closes algorithm-to-kind; §2.3.3 narrows the host record; §2.3.4 defines `hardened-backend-version-v1`. Schema relations and `check_tcb_record` / `check_claim_qualification` enforce all four.
892-- DECISION: the tree construction hashes the entry kind (`D`/`F`/`L`) alongside path and payload. Mode, owner, timestamp, ACL and xattr stay out because they change without changing what the component does; the kind stays in because it changes what the component *is* — which is what makes a symbolic link replaced by a regular file holding the referent's exact bytes a different tree rather than the same one.
893-- DECISION: `host.kind` narrowed to a constant `operating-system` and `hypervisor` deleted rather than left unenforced. Every backend the revision declares is an OS-kernel mechanism, and §2.2 already forces a hypervisor-supplied backend to mint a new profile and execution-policy identity — so the value could never legally appear, and keeping it only offered a way to detach the observed host from its platform.
894-- DECISION: version comparison needed a grammar before it could exist. `hardened-backend-version-v1` is `series "-" number(.number){0,3}` with the series closed against the backend; missing components are zero; components compare as integers. Cross-series comparison is *invalid*, not lower or higher. The lexical trap is the point: `cgroup2-6.9` sorts after `cgroup2-6.10` and is below it.
895-- FINDING: writing mutation probes found two defects in the fix itself. Widening the `hardenedHostIdentityValueV1` enum was undetected because the platform relation still covered the three declared platforms — a backstop nothing proved; `validate_schema_closed_value_sets` now pins every closed value set and requires each relation to branch on exactly the declared members. And the version pattern accepted `"sandbox-2.0\n"`: Python's `re` lets `$` match before a trailing newline, so the schema passed while only the conformance validator caught it. Pattern tail is now `(?![\s\S])`; Go RE2 was already correct.
