1866-- ROOT CAUSE: Windows `os.DirEntry.stat()` intentionally reports zero `st_dev`/`st_ino`, so comparing its cached record with physical `os.lstat()`/`os.fstat()` identities rejected every ordinary file as mutated. NTFS also interprets `bad:name` as a named data stream and exposes only base entry `bad` to directory enumeration.
1867-- FIX: the path scanner now records every descendant with no-follow `os.lstat()` while retaining the existing open/read/after-read physical identity checks. A Windows-only boundary enumerates `$DATA` streams with `FindFirstStreamW`/`FindNextStreamW`, accepts only the unnamed default stream, and fails closed on named streams or unexpected enumeration errors for the root and every descendant.
1868-- REGRESSION: a platform-independent test prevents reuse of cached `DirEntry` metadata; a Win32 API seam proves default-stream filtering and handle closure; the native NTFS case proves a hidden named stream is rejected. The pre-fix physical-stat regression exited 1 as expected; the corrected source-boundary suite exited 0 with 19 passed / 1 Windows-only skip.
